          "tz": 0
        },
        "subject": "LU-9859 target: restore memalloc flags whenever they are saved",
        "message": "LU-9859 target: restore memalloc flags whenever they are saved\n\ntgt_brw_write() starts with mpflags = 1 and calls\nmemalloc_noreclaim_restore() only if mpflags is non-zero.  Before\nLinux 6.9, memalloc_noreclaim_save() returns the old PF_MEMALLOC\nbit, usually 0, so PF_MEMALLOC stays set on the OST thread after a\nwrite from a local client under memory pressure.  Every other write\ncalls memalloc_noreclaim_restore(1), which sets bit 0 of\ncurrent->flags, PF_VCPU, so the kernel accounts the system time of\nthe thread as guest time.  Since 6.9 the saved value is the bits\nthat the save set, and the restore clears them, so no test of the\nsaved value is right for both.  ksocknal_send() restores the flags\nonly if mpflag is 0, which since 6.9 leaves PF_MEMALLOC set on the\nthread after it sends a message with msg_vmflush.\n\nTrack whether the flags were saved and restore them whenever they\nwere, as osc_build_rpc() and kgnilnd_send() do.  Also take the\nOBD_FAIL_OST_ENOSPC_VALID error of tgt_brw_write() through the\nrestore.\n\nTest-Parameters: trivial\nFixes: a35d4814f844 (\"LU-9859 libcfs: replace memory_presure functions by standard interfaces\")\nSigned-off-by: Andreas Dilger <adilger@thelustrecollective.com>\nAssisted-by: ClaudeCode:claude-opus-5-5 ltvm\nChange-Id: I4b5814dfd929138bd83460ff2b5028d093b3b0b0\nReviewed-on: https://review.whamcloud.com/c/fs/lustre-release/+/70022\nTested-by: jenkins <devops@whamcloud.com>\nReviewed-by: Timothy Day <timday@thelustrecollective.com>\nReviewed-by: Oleg Drokin <green@whamcloud.com>\nTested-by: Maloo <maloo@whamcloud.com>\nReviewed-by: Arshad Hussain <arshad.hussain@aeoncomputing.com>\n"
      },
      "branch": "refs/heads/master"
    }
  },
